blocks.en:
An important series of classical examples of Dirichlet problems come from electrostatics. Without going into any of the physics, let us assume that we wish to know a scalar quantity $u$ called the \emph{electrostatic potential} or more commonly the \emph{voltage} in a domain $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^3$. We will assume that the boundary of the domain is grounded; i.e.,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:electrostatic-grounded-dirichlet-boundary}
u(\bm{x})=0\quad\text{for }\bm{x}\in\partial\Omega.
\end{equation}
Within $\Omega$ there is a distribution of charge $\rho:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}$, and this charge ``generates\textquotesingle\textquotesingle\ a voltage through the formula
\begin{equation}\label{eq:electrostatic-charge-poisson-equation}
-\Delta u=\rho.
\end{equation}
The solution of this class of problems is the subject of classical potential theory, and many of the techniques of the classical theory (eigenfunction expansion, Green\textquotesingle s functions) are included in the modern theory as well. However, the classical and modern theory share the same approach only when we are looking for classical ($\rho\in C_b(\Omega)$) or strong ($\rho\in L^2(\Omega)$ or piecewise continuous) solutions.

The classical and modern theories take a very different approach in dealing with charge distributions that occur on surfaces; i.e., situations where $S\subset\Omega$ is a smooth surface and a charge distribution $\omega:S\to\mathbb{R}$ is defined. (We assume that either $\omega\in C_b(S)$ or $\omega\in L^2(S)$.) Of course, since $\omega$ is defined only on a surface (a set of measure zero to readers who have had measure theory) the differential equation $-\Delta u=\omega$ does not make sense either classically or as the identification of equivalence classes of sequences in $L^2(\Omega)$. In the modern theory the situation is very clear: although $\omega$ is not in $L^2(\Omega)$, we can use it to define a perfectly nice functional in $H^{-1}(\Omega)$ through the formula
\begin{equation}\label{eq:surface-charge-negative-sobolev-functional}
(\omega,\phi)=\int_S\omega(\bm{x})\phi(\bm{x})\,\mathrm{d}a(\bm{x}),
\end{equation}
for every $\phi\in H_0^1(\Omega)$. (Recall that the trace theorem implies that $\phi\in L^2(S)$.) Here $\mathrm{d}a(\bm{x})$ indicates differential area at $\bm{x}\in S$. Thus, the modern theory would simply have us look for a (weak) solution $u\in H_0^1(\Omega)$ of the variational problem
\begin{equation}\label{eq:surface-charge-electrostatic-variational-problem}
B[v,u]:=\int_\Omega\nabla v\cdot\nabla u\,\mathrm{d}\bm{x}=(\omega,v)
\end{equation}
for all $v\in H_0^1(\Omega)$. As we shall see below, this problem is well-posed. The classical theory solves this problem (and some other similar ones) using the theory of single and double layer potentials: essentially integral operators defined using singular surface integrals. As is so often the case, the classical theory lacks much of the conceptual unity of the modern theory, but provides much more detailed information in special (though often the most important) cases. We will not go into the results of classical potential theory in this book, but the reader is encouraged to read \emph{Foundations of Potential Theory} by O.D. Kellogg \cite{Ke} as a good starting point for more information on this subject.

blocks.zh:
一系列重要的经典 Dirichlet 问题例子来自静电学. 不涉及任何物理细节, 假设希望知道区域 $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^3$ 中一个称为\emph{静电势}, 或更常称为\emph{电压}的标量 $u$. 假设区域的边界接地; 即
\begin{equation}\label{eq:electrostatic-grounded-dirichlet-boundary}
u(\bm{x})=0\quad\text{for }\bm{x}\in\partial\Omega.
\end{equation}
在 $\Omega$ 内有电荷分布 $\rho:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}$, 这些电荷通过公式
\begin{equation}\label{eq:electrostatic-charge-poisson-equation}
-\Delta u=\rho.
\end{equation}
``产生\textquotesingle\textquotesingle\ 电压. 这类问题的求解是经典位势理论的研究内容, 经典理论的许多方法 (特征函数展开, Green 函数) 也包含在现代理论中. 然而, 只有当寻找经典解 ($\rho\in C_b(\Omega)$) 或强解 ($\rho\in L^2(\Omega)$ 或分段连续) 时, 经典理论与现代理论才采用相同的方法.

对于分布在曲面上的电荷, 经典理论与现代理论采取截然不同的方法; 即 $S\subset\Omega$ 为光滑曲面, 并在其上定义电荷分布 $\omega:S\to\mathbb{R}$ 的情形. (假设 $\omega\in C_b(S)$ 或 $\omega\in L^2(S)$.) 当然, 由于 $\omega$ 只定义在一个曲面上 (对学过测度论的读者而言, 这是零测集), 微分方程 $-\Delta u=\omega$ 无论按经典意义还是按 $L^2(\Omega)$ 中序列等价类的等同来理解, 都没有意义. 在现代理论中, 情况十分清楚: 虽然 $\omega$ 不属于 $L^2(\Omega)$, 却可以用公式
\begin{equation}\label{eq:surface-charge-negative-sobolev-functional}
(\omega,\phi)=\int_S\omega(\bm{x})\phi(\bm{x})\,\mathrm{d}a(\bm{x}),
\end{equation}
对每个 $\phi\in H_0^1(\Omega)$ 定义一个很好的 $H^{-1}(\Omega)$ 中的泛函. (回想迹定理表明 $\phi\in L^2(S)$.) 这里 $\mathrm{d}a(\bm{x})$ 表示 $\bm{x}\in S$ 处的面积微元. 因而现代理论只要求寻找变分问题
\begin{equation}\label{eq:surface-charge-electrostatic-variational-problem}
B[v,u]:=\int_\Omega\nabla v\cdot\nabla u\,\mathrm{d}\bm{x}=(\omega,v)
\end{equation}
对所有 $v\in H_0^1(\Omega)$ 成立的一个 (弱) 解 $u\in H_0^1(\Omega)$. 如下文所示, 该问题适定. 经典理论利用单层势与双层势理论来求解这一问题 (及其他一些类似问题): 其本质是由奇异曲面积分定义的积分算子. 正如经常出现的情形一样, 经典理论缺乏现代理论在概念上的许多统一性, 但在特殊情形 (尽管往往是最重要的情形) 中提供了详细得多的信息. 本书不讨论经典位势理论的结果, 但建议读者阅读 O.D. Kellogg 的 \emph{Foundations of Potential Theory} \cite{Ke}, 作为进一步了解这一主题的良好起点.
